263
00:10:44,144 --> 00:10:45,145
I stole her innocence?

264
00:10:45,354 --> 00:10:46,563
Well, what about my innocence?

265
00:10:46,772 --> 00:10:48,291
I was never the same
after we broke up.

266
00:10:48,315 --> 00:10:49,066
Adult Sheldon: Two years later,

267
00:10:49,274 --> 00:10:51,652
Mr. Givens became
the high school principal,

268
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:54,822
which might be why Ms. Macelroy
became morbidly obese.

269
00:10:55,030 --> 00:10:57,407
I had the brains, I had the
charm, and I don't like to brag,

270
00:10:57,616 --> 00:10:59,368
but I was pretty easy
on the eyes back then.

271
00:10:59,576 --> 00:11:01,620
That woman got herself
one heck of a package.

272
00:11:01,829 --> 00:11:03,705
But some people don't
want to be satisfied.

273
00:11:05,958 --> 00:11:07,334
Is that any good?

274
00:11:08,127 --> 00:11:09,127
I'm afraid not.

275
00:11:09,169 --> 00:11:10,921
I failed to make
a single friend.

276
00:11:11,130 --> 00:11:13,966
That sucks. I have a tough time
making friends, too.

277
00:11:14,174 --> 00:11:16,468
The worst part is, it
was important to my mom.

278
00:11:16,718 --> 00:11:19,471
My parents pressure me about
making friends all the time.

279
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:21,640
So you understand
what I'm going through.

280
00:11:23,058 --> 00:11:25,352
Oh, I read that book.
It's excellent.

281
00:11:25,853 --> 00:11:26,979
Are you into rocketry?

282
00:11:27,187 --> 00:11:29,314
I started with water propulsion,

283
00:11:29,523 --> 00:11:30,763
worked my way up to solid fuel,

284
00:11:30,899 --> 00:11:33,735
then went back to water after
I set our garage on fire.

285
00:11:34,194 --> 00:11:36,572
- Nice.
- I also tried to get some uranium

286
00:11:36,780 --> 00:11:40,033
and build an atomic engine,
but that stuff's hard to find.

287
00:11:41,285 --> 00:11:42,786
Probably for the best.

288
00:11:42,995 --> 00:11:45,080
Well, good luck on your
quest to find a friend.

289
00:11:45,455 --> 00:11:46,455
You, too.

290
00:11:52,838 --> 00:11:55,174
If you haven't found one
yet, I have good news.

291
00:11:58,468 --> 00:12:01,096
Mom, what do you know
about the drug Prozac?

292
00:12:01,638 --> 00:12:02,638
Not much.

293
00:12:02,681 --> 00:12:04,808
Supposed to make
people happier. Why?

294
00:12:05,767 --> 00:12:06,435
I was just thinking

295
00:12:06,643 --> 00:12:08,812
some of my teachers
might benefit from it.

296
00:12:10,439 --> 00:12:11,064
Speaking of which,

297
00:12:11,273 --> 00:12:13,400
you'll be happy to know
I made a friend today.

298
00:12:14,193 --> 00:12:15,193
0h.

299
00:12:16,361 --> 00:12:17,738
Well, that's nice.

300
00:12:17,946 --> 00:12:19,865
But, as I said,
this isn't for me.

301
00:12:20,073 --> 00:12:21,200
It's for you.

302
00:12:22,409 --> 00:12:23,577
He made a friend!

303
00:12:23,827 --> 00:12:24,827
He did it!

304
00:12:24,912 --> 00:12:27,664
Our little boy has got a friend.

305
00:12:27,873 --> 00:12:29,458
- Who is it?
- Who cares?

306
00:12:29,666 --> 00:12:31,752
It's a human being.
It's a warm body.

307
00:12:32,419 --> 00:12:33,670
Sheldon: Mom?

308
00:12:33,879 --> 00:12:34,463
Yes, honey?

309
00:12:34,671 --> 00:12:36,256
- Can I come in?
- Of course.

310
00:12:36,465 --> 00:12:37,883
Be cool.

311
00:12:38,967 --> 00:12:40,677
- What's up, sweetie?
- I was wondering

312
00:12:40,886 --> 00:12:43,180
when I could continue
my rocketry hobby.

313
00:12:43,513 --> 00:12:45,390
Uh, Sheldon,
we've been through this.

314
00:12:45,599 --> 00:12:47,202
You can build them.
You just can't launch them.

315
00:12:47,226 --> 00:12:49,102
But launching them
is the fun part.

316
00:12:49,394 --> 00:12:50,103
Tell that to the family
of squirrels

317
00:12:50,312 --> 00:12:52,689
- who died in the fire.
- Okay.

318
00:12:52,898 --> 00:12:55,359
It was just something I was
hoping to do with my new friend.

319
00:12:55,776 --> 00:12:56,568
Hang on.

320
00:12:56,777 --> 00:12:58,028
Uh, yes.

321
00:12:58,403 --> 00:12:59,403
It's fine.

322
00:12:59,446 --> 00:13:00,239
Ban is lifted.

323
00:13:00,447 --> 00:13:02,157
- Mary...
- It's for his friend.

324
00:13:02,366 --> 00:13:03,533
He's got a friend.

325
00:13:04,368 --> 00:13:05,768
Besides,
those could have been rats.

326
00:13:05,911 --> 00:13:07,663
Their bodies
were so badly burned.

327
00:13:08,455 --> 00:13:10,707
I thought we were being cool,
but okay.

328
00:13:11,416 --> 00:13:12,459
Hey, here's an idea.

329
00:13:12,668 --> 00:13:14,920
Why don't you invite your
new buddy over for dinner?

330
00:13:15,170 --> 00:13:16,463
- Why?
- Well, honey,

331
00:13:16,672 --> 00:13:19,758
um, that is what friends do.

332
00:13:19,967 --> 00:13:21,301
My mother asked me to ask you

333
00:13:21,510 --> 00:13:23,679
if you'd like to
join us for dinner.

334
00:13:24,054 --> 00:13:26,974
- Why?
- I was hoping you'd know.

335
00:13:33,313 --> 00:13:34,313
Mary: He's here!

336
00:13:34,439 --> 00:13:36,733
Everybody, stay calm!
Just a normal day,

337
00:13:36,942 --> 00:13:39,382
- just a normal dinner.
- Missy: Can I eat in front of the TV?

338
00:13:39,528 --> 00:13:41,528
Mary: No, you can eat
in front of Sheldon's friend.

339
00:13:41,655 --> 00:13:43,573
- Missy: Aw, fudge.
- I know what "fudge" means,

340
00:13:43,782 --> 00:13:45,784
and you are right
on the edge, young lady.

341
00:13:46,660 --> 00:13:47,828
Welcome.

342
00:13:58,046 --> 00:13:59,047
George sr.: 80, uh...

343
00:13:59,298 --> 00:14:01,174
Tam. What kind of name is that?

344
00:14:01,550 --> 00:14:03,010
Vietnamese. Sir.

345
00:14:03,218 --> 00:14:05,138
Sure. You know, I spent
a little time over there.

346
00:14:05,429 --> 00:14:06,430
Army.

347
00:14:09,599 --> 00:14:11,226
Your mom's name isn't
Kim-Lee, is it?

348
00:14:11,518 --> 00:14:12,811
No. Sir.

349
00:14:13,270 --> 00:14:14,270
Good.

350
00:14:14,438 --> 00:14:16,898
I mean, you know,
it's a small country.

351
00:14:18,150 --> 00:14:18,942
So...

352
00:14:19,151 --> 00:14:20,402
Mary, how's that food coming?

353
00:14:20,610 --> 00:14:21,610
Mary: Almost.

354
00:14:21,737 --> 00:14:23,572
So, Vietnam, like in Rambo.

355
00:14:23,947 --> 00:14:25,699
- Yes.
- That's a cool movie.

356
00:14:26,033 --> 00:14:27,033
Yes.

357
00:14:27,075 --> 00:14:28,075
Are you in it?

358
00:14:28,493 --> 00:14:29,911
-Hmm.

359
00:14:31,038 --> 00:14:32,956
Why would you think
you knew his mom?

360
00:14:33,457 --> 00:14:34,750
Mary: All right, tam.

361
00:14:34,958 --> 00:14:37,711
I decided I was gonna make
you a real Texas dinner.

362
00:14:38,045 --> 00:14:39,379
Barbecued chicken and brisket.

363
00:14:39,588 --> 00:14:40,380
Thank you.

364
00:14:40,589 --> 00:14:42,109
Well, I figured
you were probably tired

365
00:14:42,215 --> 00:14:44,009
of stuff wiggling around
on your plate.

366
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:46,136
Okay, let's say grace.

367
00:14:46,345 --> 00:14:47,554
Now, tam, when I say "Jesus,"

368
00:14:47,763 --> 00:14:49,848
feel free to say the word
"Buddha" in your head.

369
00:14:50,057 --> 00:14:51,058
I'm actually catholic.

370
00:14:51,308 --> 00:14:53,935
Oh. Well, that's too bad.

371
00:14:54,519 --> 00:14:55,771
Thank you, god, for this food,

372
00:14:55,979 --> 00:14:57,332
and bless the hands
that prepared it.

373
00:14:57,356 --> 00:15:00,359
And thank you so much
for Sheldon's new friend.

374
00:15:00,567 --> 00:15:01,985
All: Amen.

375
00:15:03,403 --> 00:15:05,238
So, tam, tell us
about your family.

376
00:15:05,447 --> 00:15:06,865
What brings y'all to Texas?

377
00:15:07,074 --> 00:15:08,950
Well, after the American war...

378
00:15:09,159 --> 00:15:10,285
You mean the Vietnam war.

379
00:15:10,494 --> 00:15:11,828
We call it the American war.

380
00:15:12,371 --> 00:15:13,121
- Hmm.
- Anyway,

381
00:15:13,330 --> 00:15:14,956
after the war, my
father was sent

382
00:15:15,165 --> 00:15:16,333
to a reeducation camp

383
00:15:16,541 --> 00:15:17,810
because he fought
on the wrong side.

384
00:15:17,834 --> 00:15:18,834
You mean our side.

385
00:15:19,711 --> 00:15:21,296
I was trying to be nice.

386
00:15:21,630 --> 00:15:23,215
So, for many years,

387
00:15:23,423 --> 00:15:24,883
my mother and my sisters and I

388
00:15:25,092 --> 00:15:26,092
were very poor

389
00:15:26,426 --> 00:15:28,428
and very often
didn't have much to eat.

390
00:15:28,637 --> 00:15:30,514
Then, when he was released...

391
00:15:30,722 --> 00:15:32,557
What'd they teach him
at the reeducation camp?

392
00:15:32,766 --> 00:15:33,850
How to be a communist.

393
00:15:34,059 --> 00:15:35,519
Cool, like in Rambo.

394
00:15:37,396 --> 00:15:38,146
When he was released,

395
00:15:38,355 --> 00:15:40,232
we escaped on a small boat

396
00:15:40,690 --> 00:15:43,902
and spent many weeks at sea
dodging Cambodian pirates

397
00:15:44,111 --> 00:15:45,445
until we reached Thailand.

398
00:15:45,862 --> 00:15:48,407
Oh, that's supposed to be
a beautiful country.

399
00:15:48,782 --> 00:15:50,742
- Lovely beaches.
- Tam: I wouldn't know.

400
00:15:50,992 --> 00:15:53,161
We were forced to live
in a refugee camp,

401
00:15:53,453 --> 00:15:56,123
where the only thing we had
to eat were pigeons and rats.

402
00:15:56,373 --> 00:15:58,583
Ha! That's a job for hot sauce.

403
00:16:00,585 --> 00:16:01,294
Finally,

404
00:16:01,545 --> 00:16:03,213
we were allowed
to come to United States

405
00:16:03,422 --> 00:16:05,132
and start over in galveston.

406
00:16:05,340 --> 00:16:07,926
My father saved money
and bought his own shrimp boat.

407
00:16:08,760 --> 00:16:10,846
There's a happy ending. Huh?

408
00:16:11,054 --> 00:16:12,848
See, kids?
When the going gets tough,

409
00:16:13,056 --> 00:16:15,058
- america provides.
- Tam: It did.

410
00:16:15,267 --> 00:16:18,103
Until the ku klux klan
burned our boat

411
00:16:18,311 --> 00:16:19,646
and chased us away.

412
00:16:20,522 --> 00:16:22,107
You gonna put a good spin
on that one?

413
00:16:22,607 --> 00:16:23,817
So we came to medford

414
00:16:24,025 --> 00:16:25,861
and opened up
a convenience store.

415
00:16:26,069 --> 00:16:28,572
My parents work 16 hours a day,

416
00:16:28,780 --> 00:16:30,532
seven days a week

417
00:16:30,740 --> 00:16:32,451
for very little money.

418
00:16:38,123 --> 00:16:39,916
Well, that was depressing.

419
00:16:46,590 --> 00:16:48,800
I don't understand
why it didn't launch.

420
00:16:49,009 --> 00:16:50,010
Me, neither.

421
00:16:50,218 --> 00:16:53,263
Evil Nazi scientists
did it 50 years ago.

422
00:16:53,889 --> 00:16:54,931
How hard could it be?

423
00:17:00,187 --> 00:17:01,187
Can I help you?

424
00:17:01,354 --> 00:17:03,565
Sorry to bother you, ma'am. FBI.

425
00:17:03,773 --> 00:17:05,650
We're looking
for a Sheldon Lee Cooper.

426
00:17:07,068 --> 00:17:07,819
George!

427
00:17:08,028 --> 00:17:09,613
George sr.: I'm on the can.

428
00:17:09,863 --> 00:17:11,281
Now!

429
00:17:12,949 --> 00:17:14,075
- What?
- These gentlemen

430
00:17:14,284 --> 00:17:16,912
are with the FBI.
They want to talk to Sheldon.

431
00:17:17,913 --> 00:17:18,913
What?

432
00:17:18,997 --> 00:17:19,997
We... uh...

433
00:17:20,123 --> 00:17:22,125
Y-You fellas
must have made a mistake.

434
00:17:22,334 --> 00:17:23,334
Sheldon's nine.

435
00:17:23,460 --> 00:17:24,729
Well, someone living
at this address

436
00:17:24,753 --> 00:17:27,005
recently called a mining
operation in Canada

437
00:17:27,214 --> 00:17:28,381
and tried to buy uranium.

438
00:17:28,924 --> 00:17:30,383
Okay, maybe it's not a mistake.

439
00:17:30,592 --> 00:17:32,010
It's ridiculous.

440
00:17:32,427 --> 00:17:33,467
Let's just go talk to him.

441
00:17:33,553 --> 00:17:34,137
Huh?

442
00:17:34,346 --> 00:17:36,181
He's just a little,
itty-bitty thing.

443
00:17:36,389 --> 00:17:37,557
Really, he's harmless.

444
00:17:42,062 --> 00:17:43,605
Sheldon: Sorry!

445
00:17:44,439 --> 00:17:45,690
He's in the garage.

